        }
      ],
      "ihh:evidence": [
        {
          "ihh:source": {
            "@id": "ihh:MUI-S-0024"
          },
          "ihh:quote": "1913\nApril 21 : John Muir turns 75 years old\nThe battle for Hetch Hetchy is lost; Hetch Hetchy Valley is granted to San Francisco for a water reservoir\nStory of my Boyhood and Youth is published\nAugust: visits the Harriman family as their guest at Harriman's Idaho ranch retreat near Island Park, now a state park: Harriman State Park - Today, a trail at the park has been named for Muir, the John Muir ADA (American Disabilities Act) Compliant Interpretive Trail and fishing platform - -a three-quarter-mile loop trail inspired by John Muir's August 1913 \"Island Park\" journal.\nOn the way to Island Park, Muir visited the Mormon Tabernacle in Salt Lake City where he heard \"memorable organ music,\" especially \"Nearer, my God to Thee,\" which he described as \"so devout, so sweet, so whispering low.\" (John Muir's August 1913 \"Island Park\" Idaho journal.)\nMuir is awarded honorary Doctor of Laws degree by the University of California",
          "ihh:locator": "1913"
        }
      ],
      "ihh:extractor": {
